      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "cec187ee-e1bb-59f5-9fa5-c6e48a1dca41",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Second Battle of Guam, First Battle of Guam, Battle of Antietam, Maryland Campaign\n- Places: Guam, Mariana Islands, Sharpsburg, Maryland, Antietam Creek, Agat Invasion Beach\n- Organizations: United States, Japan\n- Topics: Pacific campaign, World War II\n\nFacts:\n- In 1941, Japanese forces captured Guam from the United States in the First Battle of Guam during the Pacific campaign of World War II.\n- On September 17, 1862, the Battle of Antietam, also known in the South as the Battle of Sharpsburg, was fought near Sharpsburg, Maryland, and Antietam Creek as part of the Maryland Campaign.\n- From 21 July to 10 August 1944, the Second Battle of Guam was the American recapture of the Japanese-held U.S. territory of Guam in the Mariana Islands.\n- In 1944, the beaches of Agat were one of the landing sites of American forces in the Battle of Guam, during which the island was retaken from occupying Japanese forces.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "43f1e822-70de-5449-96fd-242616cb9421",
        "text": "Page: Battle of Guam (1944). Sentence: The Second Battle of Guam (21 July – 10 August 1944) was the American recapture of the Japanese-held island of Guam, a U.S. territory in the Mariana Islands captured by the Japanese from the U.S. in the 1941 First Battle of Guam during the Pacific campaign of World War II.\nPage: Battle of Antietam. Sentence: The Battle of Antietam , also known as the Battle of Sharpsburg, particularly in the South, was fought on September 17, 1862, near Sharpsburg, Maryland and Antietam Creek as part of the Maryland Campaign.\nPage: Agat Invasion Beach. Sentence: The beaches of Agat were one of the landing sites of American forces in the 1944 Battle of Guam, in which the island was retaken from occupying Japanese forces.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
